IMPORT prelude
IMPORT `eoa-dates`

-- Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) -- the complaint to the Commissioner for Equal Opportunity and the inquiry by the
-- State Administrative Tribunal, from the seat of the complainant.
--
-- SEAT: the person who says they were discriminated against, harassed or victimised unlawfully (in this encoding's
-- focus, an employee or job applicant) and complains to the Commissioner: "Who may lodge my complaint?", "By when?",
-- "Can it still be accepted late?", "Can I complain about sexual harassment I have taken to the industrial system?",
-- "Can it lapse, and can I revive it?", "If it is dismissed, can I still get it to the Tribunal, and by when?", "Will it
-- go to the Tribunal?", "Will the Commissioner help me there?", "What can the Tribunal order, and how much can it award
-- me?", "Whom may I tell about my sexual harassment complaint?". The Commissioner, the Tribunal and the Minister get no
-- seat: their opinions and decisions are asked as facts. The respondent is not given a seat here.
--
-- Every exported question takes plain facts. No question takes a DEONTIC or an EVENT. The duties with deadlines the
-- complainant meets are regulative rules run inside questions by EVALTRACE: s 83(4) (lodge within 12 months), s 83A(2)
-- (answer a lapse warning within 21 days), s 83A(4)(d) (pursue a withdrawn or lapsed complaint afresh within 6 months)
-- and s 90(1) (require referral of a dismissed complaint within 21 days of the notice).
--
-- PROVISIONS COVERED: s 4(1) (complainant, complaint, representative complaint, respondent); ss 83, 83A, 83B (inserted
-- by the Industrial Relations Legislation Amendment Act 2024, in operation 31 January 2025), 89, 90, 91, 92, 93, 107(3),
-- (3a), (5), 108, 123, 126, 127, 134(1), 165. Out, with reasons, in NOTES.md.
--
-- Edition: Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA), compilation retrieved 7 September 2026 from legislation.wa.gov.au (mrdoc
-- 48472), compilation table ending with No. 43 of 2024 (in operation 31 January 2025); deposited at
-- ../../registers/source-bundle/mrdoc_48472.txt.
--
-- Sourced from the Western Australian Legislation website, State of Western Australia, CC BY 4.0 (SOURCE-LICENSE.md).
-- BASELINE ENCODING. Not for public use as advice. Answers follow the rulings on negatives of 2026-11-04 and 2026-11-05
-- (NOTES.md): "(the text's no)" marks the text's own no; "NOTHING-kind" marks a case the text does not answer.

§ `Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA): the complaint and the Tribunal, from the complainant's seat`

§§ `s 83(1), (1a), (3), (6) -- Who may lodge my complaint?`

-- s 83(1): "A complaint alleging that a person has committed a contravention of this Act, other than a contravention in
-- respect of which a specific penalty is imposed, may be lodged in writing with the Commissioner by -- (a) a person on
-- the person's own behalf or on the person's own behalf and the behalf of other persons; or (b) 2 or more persons on
-- their own behalf or on behalf of themselves and other persons; or (c) a trade union of which a person or persons
-- aggrieved by the alleged contravention is a member or are members on behalf of that person or those persons."
-- (1a): for a person having an impairment, also "(a) where the person having the impairment is unable to write or sign
-- his or her name, by a person authorised to lodge the complaint by the person having the impairment; or (b) where the
-- person having the impairment is unable to authorise a person to lodge the complaint, by any person who satisfies the
-- Commissioner of the proper interest of that person in the care and protection of the person having the impairment."
-- (6): "trade union means an organisation of employees or an organisation of employers."
DECLARE `Who would lodge the complaint` IS ONE OF
    `I would, for myself (and perhaps for others too)`
    `I would, together with one or more others`
    `a trade union of which I am a member`
    `a person I authorise, as my impairment means I cannot write or sign my name`
    `a person with a proper interest in my care and protection, as my impairment means I cannot authorise anyone`
    `someone else`

DECLARE `May the complaint be lodged` IS ONE OF
    `yes` HAS `under` IS A STRING
    `no: a contravention for which a specific penalty is imposed is not one s 83 lets you complain of ("other than": the text's no, fork F5)`
    `NOTHING-kind` HAS `why` IS A STRING

@export Who may lodge my complaint with the Commissioner for Equal Opportunity under the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) (s 83(1), (1a), (3), (6))? A complaint must be in writing. You may lodge it yourself (for yourself and others too), with others, or through your trade union; if you have an impairment and cannot write or sign, a person you authorise may lodge it, and if you cannot authorise anyone, a person the Commissioner is satisfied has a proper interest in your care. It may be a representative complaint.
GIVEN `who would lodge it` IS A `Who would lodge the complaint` @desc Who would lodge the complaint. One of: I would, for myself (and perhaps for others too); I would, together with one or more others; a trade union of which I am a member; a person I authorise, as my impairment means I cannot write or sign my name; a person with a proper interest in my care and protection, as my impairment means I cannot authorise anyone; someone else
      `it is in writing` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Will the complaint be lodged in writing?
      `a specific penalty is imposed for it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the contravention one for which the Act imposes a specific penalty (an offence, such as obstruction or failing to attend a conference), rather than an act made unlawful (discrimination, harassment, victimisation)?
      `the Commissioner is satisfied of the proper interest` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a person lodging for someone who cannot authorise anyone: is the Commissioner satisfied of that person's proper interest in the care and protection of the person with the impairment?
GIVETH A `May the complaint be lodged`
`s 83 -- who may lodge my complaint` `who would lodge it` `it is in writing` `a specific penalty is imposed for it` `the Commissioner is satisfied of the proper interest` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `a specific penalty is imposed for it`
        THEN `no: a contravention for which a specific penalty is imposed is not one s 83 lets you complain of ("other than": the text's no, fork F5)`
        IF (NOT `it is in writing`)
        THEN `NOTHING-kind` "s 83(1) provides for a complaint lodged in writing; it says nothing of any other"
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER `who would lodge it`
            WHEN `I would, for myself (and perhaps for others too)` THEN `yes` "s 83(1)(a)"
            WHEN `I would, together with one or more others` THEN `yes` "s 83(1)(b)"
            WHEN `a trade union of which I am a member` THEN `yes` "s 83(1)(c)"
            WHEN `a person I authorise, as my impairment means I cannot write or sign my name` THEN `yes` "s 83(1a)(a)"
            WHEN `a person with a proper interest in my care and protection, as my impairment means I cannot authorise anyone` THEN
                IF `the Commissioner is satisfied of the proper interest`
                THEN `yes` "s 83(1a)(b)"
                ELSE `NOTHING-kind` "s 83(1a)(b) needs the Commissioner to be satisfied of the person's proper interest; until then it does not reach the case"
            WHEN `someone else` THEN `NOTHING-kind` "s 83(1) and (1a) list who may lodge; the list is not closed with 'only', but names no one else"

§§ `s 83(4), (5) -- By when must I lodge my complaint?`

-- s 83(4): "A complaint made under subsection (1) or (1a) shall be lodged within 12 months after the date on which the
-- contravention of this Act which is the subject of that complaint is alleged to have been committed." (5):
-- "Notwithstanding subsection (4), the Commissioner, on good cause being shown, may accept a complaint which is lodged
-- more than 12 months after the date referred to in that subsection." The last day is the date 12 calendar months on
-- (fork F1); for conduct over a period the person gives the last day of it (fork F6).
@export What is the last day on which I can lodge my complaint with the Commissioner for Equal Opportunity within the 12 months the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) allows (s 83(4))? After it, the Commissioner may still accept the complaint, but only on good cause being shown (s 83(5)).
GIVEN `the contravention was on` IS A DATE @desc The date on which you say the discrimination, harassment or victimisation happened (if it went on over a period, the last day of it)
GIVETH A DATE
`s 83(4) -- the last day to lodge` `the contravention was on` MEANS `the contravention was on` `plus calendar months` 12

DECLARE `Complainant` IS ONE OF `the complainant`
DECLARE `Complaint step` IS ONE OF
    `lodge the complaint with the Commissioner`
    `serve notice that I wish the Commissioner to continue`
    `pursue the complaint afresh`
    `require the Commissioner to refer the complaint to the Tribunal`

-- s 83(4) as a rule with a deadline for the complainant: lodge within 12 months, or the complaint is late and needs the
-- Commissioner's acceptance under s 83(5).
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Complainant` `Complaint step`
`s 83(4) -- lodge within 12 months` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the complainant`
    MUST `lodge the complaint with the Commissioner`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the complainant` BECAUSE "complaint lodged more than 12 months after the alleged contravention: the Commissioner may accept it only on good cause being shown (s 83(5))"

-- Runs a rule over one step taken on one day. On the stable l4 a CONSIDER on an EVALTRACE can match FULFILLED but not
-- BREACH, so FULFILLED means in time and anything else late. Not exported; it takes no DEONTIC from outside.
GIVEN `the rule` IS A DEONTIC `Complainant` `Complaint step`, `the step` IS A `Complaint step`, `the day` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `taking the step on this day keeps the rule` `the rule` `the step` `the day` IF
    CONSIDER EVALTRACE `the rule` 0 (LIST (WAITUNTIL `the day`), (EVENT `the complainant` `the step` `the day`))
    WHEN FULFILLED THEN TRUE
    OTHERWISE FALSE

GIVEN `the start` IS A DATE, `the last day` IS A DATE, `the step` IS A `Complaint step`, `the rule` IS A FUNCTION FROM NUMBER TO DEONTIC `Complainant` `Complaint step`, `the day it was done` IS A DATE
GIVETH A BOOLEAN
DECIDE `done in time` `the start` `the last day` `the step` `the rule` `the day it was done` IF
    `taking the step on this day keeps the rule` (`the rule` `the days allowed`) `the step` (`the day number of` `the day it was done` `counted from` `the start`)
    WHERE
        `the days allowed` MEANS `the number of days from` `the start` `until` `the last day`

DECLARE `Is my complaint in time` IS ONE OF
    `in time: lodged within 12 months after the alleged contravention (s 83(4))`
    `late, but the Commissioner has accepted it on good cause being shown (s 83(5))`
    `late: lodged more than 12 months after the alleged contravention, and not accepted under s 83(5) (s 83(4)'s period: the text's no)`

@export Is my complaint to the Commissioner for Equal Opportunity in time under the Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) (s 83(4), (5))? A complaint must be lodged within 12 months after the date of the alleged contravention; a later complaint may be accepted by the Commissioner on good cause being shown.
GIVEN `the contravention was on` IS A DATE @desc The date on which you say the discrimination, harassment or victimisation happened (if over a period, the last day of it)
      `lodged on` IS A DATE @desc The date you lodged, or will lodge, the written complaint with the Commissioner
      `the Commissioner accepted it on good cause shown` IS A BOOLEAN @desc If it is late: has the Commissioner accepted it, on good cause being shown for the delay?
GIVETH AN `Is my complaint in time`
`s 83(4) -- is my complaint in time` `the contravention was on` `lodged on` `the Commissioner accepted it on good cause shown` MEANS
    IF `done in time` `the contravention was on` (`s 83(4) -- the last day to lodge` `the contravention was on`) `lodge the complaint with the Commissioner` `s 83(4) -- lodge within 12 months` `lodged on`
    THEN `in time: lodged within 12 months after the alleged contravention (s 83(4))`
    ELSE IF `the Commissioner accepted it on good cause shown`
         THEN `late, but the Commissioner has accepted it on good cause being shown (s 83(5))`
         ELSE `late: lodged more than 12 months after the alleged contravention, and not accepted under s 83(5) (s 83(4)'s period: the text's no)`

§§ `s 83B -- Sexual harassment at work already taken to the industrial system`

-- s 83B(2): "A complaint cannot be made under section 83 in relation to a particular allegation -- (a) if either of the
-- following have been made under the Industrial Relations Act 1979 in relation to the allegation -- (i) a referral under
-- section 29; or (ii) an application to the industrial magistrate's court under section 83E to enforce a contravention of
-- section 51BR(1) of that Act; and (b) unless the referral or application is withdrawn or fails for want of
-- jurisdiction." (1): "particular allegation means a particular allegation by a person that the person was sexually
-- harassed in connection with work as defined in the Industrial Relations Act 1979 section 51BR(2)." (3): it "applies
-- despite any other provision of this Act". Inserted by No. 43 of 2024, in operation 31 January 2025. The Industrial
-- Relations Act 1979 terms are borrowed and asked.
DECLARE `Does s 83B stop my complaint` IS ONE OF
    `no: s 83B does not stop this complaint`
    `no: the industrial referral or application was withdrawn or failed for want of jurisdiction, so s 83B no longer stops the complaint (s 83B(2)(b))`
    `yes: a complaint cannot be made under s 83 about this allegation (s 83B(2): the text's no)`

@export I say I was sexually harassed in connection with work: can I still complain to the Commissioner for Equal Opportunity if I have taken the same allegation to the WA industrial relations system (Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) s 83B, from 31 January 2025)? Not if a referral under s 29 of the Industrial Relations Act 1979, or an application to the industrial magistrate's court under s 83E to enforce s 51BR(1), has been made about it, unless that was withdrawn or failed for want of jurisdiction.
GIVEN `sexual harassment in connection with work` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the allegation that you were sexually harassed in connection with work, as defined in section 51BR(2) of the Industrial Relations Act 1979?
      `an industrial referral or application was made` IS A BOOLEAN @desc About that same allegation, has a referral been made under section 29 of the Industrial Relations Act 1979, or an application to the industrial magistrate's court under section 83E to enforce section 51BR(1)?
      `it was withdrawn or failed for want of jurisdiction` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Was that referral or application withdrawn, or did it fail for want of jurisdiction?
GIVETH A `Does s 83B stop my complaint`
`s 83B -- does the industrial route stop my complaint` `sexual harassment in connection with work` `an industrial referral or application was made` `it was withdrawn or failed for want of jurisdiction` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT (`sexual harassment in connection with work` AND `an industrial referral or application was made`))
        THEN `no: s 83B does not stop this complaint`
        IF `it was withdrawn or failed for want of jurisdiction`
        THEN `no: the industrial referral or application was withdrawn or failed for want of jurisdiction, so s 83B no longer stops the complaint (s 83B(2)(b))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: a complaint cannot be made under s 83 about this allegation (s 83B(2): the text's no)`

§§ `s 83A -- Withdrawing, lapse and pursuing afresh`

-- s 83A(1): "A complainant may at any time between the lodging of the relevant complaint and the final disposal of that
-- complaint under this Division withdraw that complaint by notice in writing served on the Commissioner."
DECLARE `May I withdraw my complaint` IS ONE OF
    `yes: by notice in writing served on the Commissioner (s 83A(1))`
    `no: the complaint has been finally disposed of under Division 2 of Part VII (s 83A(1)'s period: the text's no)`

@export May I withdraw my complaint to the Commissioner for Equal Opportunity (Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) s 83A(1))? At any time between lodging it and its final disposal, by written notice served on the Commissioner. Once withdrawn it can be pursued again only with good cause and within 6 months (s 83A(4)).
GIVEN `it has been finally disposed of` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Commissioner finally disposed of the complaint (for example dismissed it, resolved it by conciliation or referred it to the Tribunal)?
GIVETH A `May I withdraw my complaint`
`s 83A(1) -- may I withdraw my complaint` `it has been finally disposed of` MEANS
    IF `it has been finally disposed of`
    THEN `no: the complaint has been finally disposed of under Division 2 of Part VII (s 83A(1)'s period: the text's no)`
    ELSE `yes: by notice in writing served on the Commissioner (s 83A(1))`

-- s 83A(2): if in the Commissioner's opinion the complainant is not pursuing or has abandoned the complaint, the
-- Commissioner may serve a written warning that the complaint "will, unless within a period of 21 days after that
-- service the complainant serves on the Commissioner notice in writing that the complainant wishes the Commissioner to
-- continue to investigate or otherwise deal with that complaint ..., lapse at the end of that period." (3): "If a
-- person on whom a warning is served ... does not within the period of 21 days after that service serve on the
-- Commissioner notice referred to in that subsection, the relevant complaint lapses at the end of that period."
@export The Commissioner for Equal Opportunity has warned me my complaint will lapse: what is the last day to serve my written notice that I want it to continue (Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) s 83A(2), (3))? Within 21 days after the warning was served on you; otherwise the complaint lapses at the end of that period.
GIVEN `the warning was served on` IS A DATE @desc The date the Commissioner's written warning was served on you
GIVETH A DATE
`s 83A(2) -- the last day to answer the lapse warning` `the warning was served on` MEANS `the warning was served on` `plus days` 21

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Complainant` `Complaint step`
`s 83A(2) -- answer the warning within 21 days` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the complainant`
    MUST `serve notice that I wish the Commissioner to continue`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the complainant` BECAUSE "no notice within 21 days after the warning: the complaint lapses at the end of that period (s 83A(3))"

DECLARE `Has my complaint lapsed` IS ONE OF
    `no: you served notice within the 21 days, so it does not lapse (s 83A(2))`
    `yes` HAS `it lapses at the end of` IS A DATE

@export Has my complaint to the Commissioner for Equal Opportunity lapsed after a lapse warning (Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) s 83A(2), (3))? It lapses at the end of the 21 days after the warning was served, unless within them you serve written notice that you want the Commissioner to continue.
GIVEN `the warning was served on` IS A DATE @desc The date the Commissioner's written warning was served on you
      `I served notice to continue` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you served (or will you serve) on the Commissioner written notice that you wish the Commissioner to continue to deal with the complaint?
      `my notice was served on` IS A DATE @desc If so, the date your notice was served on the Commissioner (otherwise any date)
GIVETH A `Has my complaint lapsed`
`s 83A(3) -- has my complaint lapsed` `the warning was served on` `I served notice to continue` `my notice was served on` MEANS
    IF      `I served notice to continue`
        AND `done in time` `the warning was served on` (`s 83A(2) -- the last day to answer the lapse warning` `the warning was served on`) `serve notice that I wish the Commissioner to continue` `s 83A(2) -- answer the warning within 21 days` `my notice was served on`
    THEN `no: you served notice within the 21 days, so it does not lapse (s 83A(2))`
    ELSE `yes` (`s 83A(2) -- the last day to answer the lapse warning` `the warning was served on`)

-- s 83A(4): "If a complaint -- (a) is withdrawn ...; or (b) lapses ..., the complaint shall not be pursued afresh unless
-- -- (c) the person seeking to pursue the complaint afresh satisfies the Commissioner that there is good cause for that
-- pursuit to take place; and (d) that pursuit takes place within a period of 6 months from the withdrawal or lapse of
-- the complaint." (5): if the Commissioner is so satisfied, the person may pursue it "without lodging another
-- complaint". The 6 months end on the date 6 calendar months on (fork F1).
@export My complaint to the Commissioner for Equal Opportunity was withdrawn or lapsed: what is the last day to pursue it afresh (Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) s 83A(4)(d))? Pursuit must take place within 6 months from the withdrawal or lapse, and you must satisfy the Commissioner there is good cause.
GIVEN `it was withdrawn or lapsed on` IS A DATE @desc The date the complaint was withdrawn or lapsed
GIVETH A DATE
`s 83A(4) -- the last day to pursue it afresh` `it was withdrawn or lapsed on` MEANS `it was withdrawn or lapsed on` `plus calendar months` 6

GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Complainant` `Complaint step`
`s 83A(4)(d) -- pursue afresh within 6 months` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the complainant`
    MUST `pursue the complaint afresh`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the complainant` BECAUSE "more than 6 months from the withdrawal or lapse: the complaint shall not be pursued afresh (s 83A(4)(d))"

DECLARE `May I pursue my complaint afresh` IS ONE OF
    `yes: within 6 months and with the Commissioner satisfied of good cause; no new complaint is needed (s 83A(4), (5))`
    `no: the Commissioner is not satisfied that there is good cause (s 83A(4)(c), "unless": the text's no)`
    `no: more than 6 months from the withdrawal or lapse (s 83A(4)(d)'s period: the text's no)`

@export May I pursue afresh my complaint to the Commissioner for Equal Opportunity that was withdrawn or lapsed (Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) s 83A(4), (5))? Only if you satisfy the Commissioner that there is good cause and you pursue it within 6 months from the withdrawal or lapse.
GIVEN `it was withdrawn or lapsed on` IS A DATE @desc The date the complaint was withdrawn or lapsed
      `I pursue it on` IS A DATE @desc The date you seek to pursue it afresh
      `the Commissioner is satisfied of good cause` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Commissioner satisfied that there is good cause for pursuing it afresh?
GIVETH A `May I pursue my complaint afresh`
`s 83A(4) -- may I pursue my complaint afresh` `it was withdrawn or lapsed on` `I pursue it on` `the Commissioner is satisfied of good cause` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT (`done in time` `it was withdrawn or lapsed on` (`s 83A(4) -- the last day to pursue it afresh` `it was withdrawn or lapsed on`) `pursue the complaint afresh` `s 83A(4)(d) -- pursue afresh within 6 months` `I pursue it on`))
        THEN `no: more than 6 months from the withdrawal or lapse (s 83A(4)(d)'s period: the text's no)`
        IF (NOT `the Commissioner is satisfied of good cause`)
        THEN `no: the Commissioner is not satisfied that there is good cause (s 83A(4)(c), "unless": the text's no)`
        OTHERWISE `yes: within 6 months and with the Commissioner satisfied of good cause; no new complaint is needed (s 83A(4), (5))`

§§ `ss 89, 90 -- If the Commissioner dismisses my complaint`

-- s 89(1): "Where, at any stage of an investigation, the Commissioner is satisfied that a complaint is frivolous,
-- vexatious, misconceived, lacking in substance or relates to an act that is not unlawful by reason of a provision of
-- this Act, the Commissioner may, by notice in writing addressed to the complainant, dismiss the complaint." (2): the
-- notice must give "(a) the reason for dismissing the complaint; and (b) the rights of the complainant under section 90."
DECLARE `Can my complaint be dismissed` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Commissioner may dismiss it by written notice giving the reason and your rights under s 90 (s 89)`
    `NOTHING-kind: s 89 gives no power to dismiss on these facts; the Commissioner must investigate (s 84) and try conciliation where it may succeed (s 91)`

@export Can the Commissioner for Equal Opportunity dismiss my complaint (Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) s 89)? Yes, at any stage of the investigation, if satisfied it is frivolous, vexatious, misconceived, lacking in substance, or about an act the Act does not make unlawful; the written notice must give the reason and your right to have it referred to the Tribunal.
GIVEN `the Commissioner is satisfied of a ground` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Commissioner satisfied that the complaint is frivolous, vexatious, misconceived, lacking in substance, or relates to an act that is not unlawful under the Act?
GIVETH A `Can my complaint be dismissed`
`s 89 -- can my complaint be dismissed` `the Commissioner is satisfied of a ground` MEANS
    IF `the Commissioner is satisfied of a ground`
    THEN `yes: the Commissioner may dismiss it by written notice giving the reason and your rights under s 90 (s 89)`
    ELSE `NOTHING-kind: s 89 gives no power to dismiss on these facts; the Commissioner must investigate (s 84) and try conciliation where it may succeed (s 91)`

-- s 90(1): "Where the Commissioner has given a complainant a notice under section 89, the complainant may, within 21 days
-- after the receipt of that notice, by notice in writing served on the Commissioner, require the Commissioner to refer
-- the complaint to the Tribunal." (2): "On receipt of a notice under subsection (1), the Commissioner shall refer the
-- complaint to the Tribunal together with a report relating to the investigation".
@export My complaint was dismissed by the Commissioner for Equal Opportunity: what is the last day to require it to be referred to the State Administrative Tribunal (Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) s 90(1))? Within 21 days after you received the dismissal notice, by written notice served on the Commissioner.
GIVEN `I received the dismissal notice on` IS A DATE @desc The date you received the Commissioner's written notice dismissing your complaint
GIVETH A DATE
`s 90(1) -- the last day to require referral` `I received the dismissal notice on` MEANS `I received the dismissal notice on` `plus days` 21

-- s 90(1) is a permission ("may"), but its time is fixed; as a rule with a deadline it is written MUST so that missing it
-- shows. Missing it is no breach of a duty: it means s 90 no longer gives the right.
GIVEN `the days allowed` IS A NUMBER
GIVETH A DEONTIC `Complainant` `Complaint step`
`s 90(1) -- require referral within 21 days` `the days allowed` MEANS
    PARTY `the complainant`
    MUST `require the Commissioner to refer the complaint to the Tribunal`
    WITHIN `the days allowed`
    HENCE FULFILLED
    LEST BREACH BY `the complainant` BECAUSE "notice served more than 21 days after receiving the dismissal notice: s 90 gives no right to referral (s 90(1))"

DECLARE `Must my dismissed complaint be referred` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Commissioner must refer it to the Tribunal with a report on the investigation (s 90(2))`
    `no: the notice was served more than 21 days after you received the dismissal notice (s 90(1)'s period: the text's no)`

@export My complaint was dismissed by the Commissioner for Equal Opportunity: if I serve written notice requiring referral, must the Commissioner refer it to the State Administrative Tribunal (Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) s 90)? Yes, if your notice is served within 21 days after you received the dismissal notice.
GIVEN `I received the dismissal notice on` IS A DATE @desc The date you received the Commissioner's written notice dismissing your complaint
      `I served my notice on` IS A DATE @desc The date your written notice requiring referral was (or will be) served on the Commissioner
GIVETH A `Must my dismissed complaint be referred`
`s 90 -- must my dismissed complaint be referred` `I received the dismissal notice on` `I served my notice on` MEANS
    IF `done in time` `I received the dismissal notice on` (`s 90(1) -- the last day to require referral` `I received the dismissal notice on`) `require the Commissioner to refer the complaint to the Tribunal` `s 90(1) -- require referral within 21 days` `I served my notice on`
    THEN `yes: the Commissioner must refer it to the Tribunal with a report on the investigation (s 90(2))`
    ELSE `no: the notice was served more than 21 days after you received the dismissal notice (s 90(1)'s period: the text's no)`

§§ `ss 91, 92, 93 -- Conciliation, and referral to the Tribunal`

-- s 91(1): "Where the Commissioner is of the opinion that a complaint other than a complaint that the Commissioner has
-- dismissed under section 89, may be resolved by conciliation, the Commissioner shall endeavour to resolve the complaint
-- by conciliation." s 93(1): "Without prejudice to the Commissioner's powers under section 89, where the Commissioner
-- -- (a) is of the opinion that a complaint cannot be resolved by conciliation; or (b) has endeavoured to resolve a
-- complaint by conciliation but has not been successful in such endeavours; or (c) is of the opinion that the nature of
-- a complaint is such that it should be referred to the Tribunal, the Commissioner shall refer the complaint to the
-- Tribunal together with a report". (3): "Evidence of anything said or done in the course of conciliation proceedings
-- under section 91 shall not be admissible in subsequent proceedings before the Tribunal."
DECLARE `Will my complaint go to the Tribunal` IS ONE OF
    `yes` HAS `under` IS A STRING
    `not by s 93: the complaint was resolved by conciliation`
    `not by s 93: it was dismissed under s 89; you may require referral within 21 days under s 90`
    `not yet: the Commissioner must try to resolve it by conciliation (s 91(1))`
    `NOTHING-kind: none of the s 93(1) occasions has arisen yet`

@export Will the Commissioner for Equal Opportunity refer my complaint to the State Administrative Tribunal (Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) ss 91, 93)? The Commissioner must try conciliation where it may succeed, and must refer the complaint if of the opinion it cannot be resolved by conciliation, if conciliation failed, or if of the opinion it should go to the Tribunal. What was said or done in conciliation cannot be used as evidence at the Tribunal (s 93(3)).
GIVEN `it was dismissed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Commissioner dismissed the complaint under s 89?
      `it was resolved by conciliation` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the complaint been resolved by conciliation?
      `the Commissioner thinks it cannot be conciliated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Commissioner of the opinion that the complaint cannot be resolved by conciliation?
      `conciliation was tried and failed` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Commissioner tried to resolve it by conciliation without success?
      `the Commissioner thinks it should go to the Tribunal` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Commissioner of the opinion that the nature of the complaint is such that it should be referred to the Tribunal?
      `the Commissioner thinks conciliation may resolve it` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is the Commissioner of the opinion that the complaint may be resolved by conciliation?
GIVETH A `Will my complaint go to the Tribunal`
`ss 91, 93 -- will my complaint go to the Tribunal` `it was dismissed` `it was resolved by conciliation` `the Commissioner thinks it cannot be conciliated` `conciliation was tried and failed` `the Commissioner thinks it should go to the Tribunal` `the Commissioner thinks conciliation may resolve it` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF `it was dismissed` THEN `not by s 93: it was dismissed under s 89; you may require referral within 21 days under s 90`
        IF `it was resolved by conciliation` THEN `not by s 93: the complaint was resolved by conciliation`
        IF `the Commissioner thinks it cannot be conciliated` THEN `yes` "s 93(1)(a): the Commissioner must refer it, with a report"
        IF `conciliation was tried and failed` THEN `yes` "s 93(1)(b): the Commissioner must refer it, with a report"
        IF `the Commissioner thinks it should go to the Tribunal` THEN `yes` "s 93(1)(c): the Commissioner must refer it, with a report"
        IF `the Commissioner thinks conciliation may resolve it` THEN `not yet: the Commissioner must try to resolve it by conciliation (s 91(1))`
        OTHERWISE `NOTHING-kind: none of the s 93(1) occasions has arisen yet`

-- s 92(1): "A complainant or respondent in conciliation proceedings before the Commissioner -- (a) is entitled to appear
-- personally or, subject to section 91(2), by an agent other than a solicitor or counsel; or (b) may, by leave of the
-- Commissioner, be represented by a solicitor or counsel." (2): "No person other than a solicitor or counsel is entitled
-- to demand or receive any fee or reward for representing a party in conciliation proceedings before the Commissioner."
DECLARE `Who appears for me in conciliation` IS ONE OF
    `myself`
    `an agent who is not a lawyer`
    `a lawyer (solicitor or counsel)`

DECLARE `May they appear for me in conciliation` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you are entitled to appear personally (s 92(1)(a))`
    `yes: you are entitled to appear by an agent other than a lawyer, who may not charge a fee or reward (s 92(1)(a), (2))`
    `yes: with the Commissioner's leave you may be represented by a lawyer (s 92(1)(b))`
    `no: a lawyer may represent you only by leave of the Commissioner (s 92(1)(b): the text's no)`

@export Who may appear for me in conciliation before the Commissioner for Equal Opportunity (Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) s 92)? You may appear yourself or by an agent who is not a lawyer (who may not charge); a lawyer may represent you only with the Commissioner's leave.
GIVEN `who appears` IS A `Who appears for me in conciliation` @desc Who would appear for you. One of: myself; an agent who is not a lawyer; a lawyer (solicitor or counsel)
      `the Commissioner gave leave` IS A BOOLEAN @desc For a lawyer: has the Commissioner given leave for you to be represented by a solicitor or counsel?
GIVETH A `May they appear for me in conciliation`
`s 92 -- may they appear for me in conciliation` `who appears` `the Commissioner gave leave` MEANS
    CONSIDER `who appears`
    WHEN `myself` THEN `yes: you are entitled to appear personally (s 92(1)(a))`
    WHEN `an agent who is not a lawyer` THEN `yes: you are entitled to appear by an agent other than a lawyer, who may not charge a fee or reward (s 92(1)(a), (2))`
    WHEN `a lawyer (solicitor or counsel)` THEN
        IF `the Commissioner gave leave`
        THEN `yes: with the Commissioner's leave you may be represented by a lawyer (s 92(1)(b))`
        ELSE `no: a lawyer may represent you only by leave of the Commissioner (s 92(1)(b): the text's no)`

-- s 93(2): "When a complaint is referred to the Tribunal under subsection (1), the Commissioner -- (a) shall, if the
-- complainant requests the Commissioner to do so, either personally or by counsel or representative assist the
-- complainant in the presentation of the case of the complainant to the Tribunal; and (b) may, if the complainant
-- requests the Commissioner to do so and the Commissioner considers it appropriate in all the circumstances (including
-- the financial circumstances of the complainant) to do so, make such contribution towards the cost of witness and
-- other expenses as is necessary". (2a), (2b): the contribution may carry conditions, including repayment.
DECLARE `Will the Commissioner help me at the Tribunal` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Commissioner must help you present your case, and may contribute to witness and other expenses (s 93(2)(a), (b))`
    `yes: the Commissioner must help you present your case (s 93(2)(a)); no contribution to expenses has been found appropriate`
    `NOTHING-kind: s 93(2) applies to a complaint referred under s 93(1) on your request; a complaint referred because you required it under s 90 is not within it (finding 6)`

@export Will the Commissioner for Equal Opportunity help me present my case at the State Administrative Tribunal (Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) s 93(2))? When the Commissioner refers a complaint under s 93(1), the Commissioner must, if you ask, assist in presenting your case, and may, if you ask and it is appropriate (including your finances), contribute to witness and other expenses, possibly on conditions such as repayment.
GIVEN `the Commissioner referred it under s 93(1)` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Did the Commissioner refer the complaint to the Tribunal under s 93(1) (conciliation impossible or failed, or the complaint should go to the Tribunal), rather than because you required it after a dismissal (s 90)?
      `I asked for help` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you asked the Commissioner to assist you?
      `the Commissioner considers a contribution appropriate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does the Commissioner consider it appropriate, in all the circumstances including your finances, to contribute to your witness and other expenses?
GIVETH A `Will the Commissioner help me at the Tribunal`
`s 93(2) -- will the Commissioner help me at the Tribunal` `the Commissioner referred it under s 93(1)` `I asked for help` `the Commissioner considers a contribution appropriate` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT (`the Commissioner referred it under s 93(1)` AND `I asked for help`))
        THEN `NOTHING-kind: s 93(2) applies to a complaint referred under s 93(1) on your request; a complaint referred because you required it under s 90 is not within it (finding 6)`
        IF `the Commissioner considers a contribution appropriate`
        THEN `yes: the Commissioner must help you present your case, and may contribute to witness and other expenses (s 93(2)(a), (b))`
        OTHERWISE `yes: the Commissioner must help you present your case (s 93(2)(a)); no contribution to expenses has been found appropriate`

§§ `ss 107, 108, 123, 126 -- At the Tribunal`

-- s 107(3): "Subject to subsection (3a), the Tribunal shall hold an inquiry into each complaint or matter referred to it
-- under section 90(2), 93(1) or subsection (1)." (3a): it "shall not hold, or shall discontinue, ... an inquiry into --
-- (a) a complaint referred to it under section 90(2) or 93(1) if the complainant notifies the Tribunal that the
-- complainant ... does not wish that inquiry to be held or to continue." (5): "No fee is payable in respect of a
-- proceeding commenced before the Tribunal under this Act." s 108: in a proceeding begun by a s 93(1) reference, the
-- complainant is the applicant. s 123: the respondent must prove any exception.
DECLARE `Will the Tribunal hold an inquiry` IS ONE OF
    `yes: the Tribunal must hold an inquiry; no fee is payable, and the respondent must prove any exception it relies on (ss 107(3), (5), 123)`
    `no: you have told the Tribunal you do not wish the inquiry to be held or to continue, so it must not hold it, or must stop (s 107(3a): the text's no)`

@export My complaint has been referred to the State Administrative Tribunal: will the Tribunal hold an inquiry, and can I stop it (Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) ss 107, 108, 123)? The Tribunal must hold an inquiry into a referred complaint, free of fees, unless you notify it that you do not wish the inquiry to be held or to continue; you are the applicant, and the respondent must prove any exception.
GIVEN `I told the Tribunal I do not wish it to go on` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you notified the Tribunal that you do not wish the inquiry to be held or to continue?
GIVETH A `Will the Tribunal hold an inquiry`
`s 107 -- will the Tribunal hold an inquiry` `I told the Tribunal I do not wish it to go on` MEANS
    IF `I told the Tribunal I do not wish it to go on`
    THEN `no: you have told the Tribunal you do not wish the inquiry to be held or to continue, so it must not hold it, or must stop (s 107(3a): the text's no)`
    ELSE `yes: the Tribunal must hold an inquiry; no fee is payable, and the respondent must prove any exception it relies on (ss 107(3), (5), 123)`

-- s 126: "The Tribunal may, on the application of the Commissioner under section 85, or on the application of a party to
-- an investigation at any time after the lodging of the complaint into which that investigation is held, make an
-- interim order to preserve -- (a) the status quo between the parties to the complaint; or (b) the rights of the parties
-- to the complaint, pending determination of the matter".
DECLARE `May I apply for an interim order` IS ONE OF
    `yes: you may apply to the Tribunal for an interim order to preserve the status quo or the parties' rights until the complaint is determined (s 126)`
    `NOTHING-kind: s 126 lets a party apply at any time after the complaint is lodged; before then it does not reach the case`

@export May I ask the State Administrative Tribunal for an interim order to protect my position while my complaint is dealt with (Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) s 126)? A party may apply at any time after the complaint is lodged, for an order preserving the status quo or the parties' rights until it is determined.
GIVEN `my complaint has been lodged` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Have you lodged your complaint with the Commissioner?
GIVETH A `May I apply for an interim order`
`s 126 -- may I apply for an interim order` `my complaint has been lodged` MEANS
    IF `my complaint has been lodged`
    THEN `yes: you may apply to the Tribunal for an interim order to preserve the status quo or the parties' rights until the complaint is determined (s 126)`
    ELSE `NOTHING-kind: s 126 lets a party apply at any time after the complaint is lodged; before then it does not reach the case`

§§ `ss 127, 134 -- What the Tribunal can order`

-- s 127: "After holding an inquiry, the Tribunal may -- (a) dismiss the complaint ...; or (b) find the complaint
-- substantiated and do any one or more of the following -- (i) except in respect of a representative complaint or a
-- matter referred to the Tribunal for inquiry as a complaint pursuant to section 107(1), order the respondent to pay to
-- the complainant damages not exceeding $40 000 by way of compensation for any loss or damage suffered by reason of the
-- respondent's conduct; or (ii) make an order enjoining the respondent from continuing or repeating any conduct
-- rendered unlawful by this Act; or (iii) except in respect of a representative complaint or a matter referred ...
-- pursuant to section 107(1), order the respondent to perform any reasonable act or course of conduct to redress any
-- loss or damage suffered by the complainant; or (iv) make an order declaring void in whole or in part and either ab
-- initio or from such other time as is specified in the order any contract or agreement made in contravention of this
-- Act; or (v) decline to take any further action in the matter."
`the damages cap` MEANS 40000

@export What orders can the State Administrative Tribunal make if it finds my complaint substantiated (Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) s 127)? Damages of up to $40,000 for your loss or damage, an order stopping the conduct, an order to do a reasonable act to redress your loss, an order voiding a contract made in breach of the Act, or no further action; damages and redress orders are not available on a representative complaint.
GIVEN `it is a representative complaint` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your complaint a representative complaint (lodged for other people as well, and treated by the Tribunal as representative)?
      `the Tribunal found it substantiated` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Tribunal, after its inquiry, found the complaint substantiated? (If not, it may dismiss it: s 127(a).)
GIVETH A LIST OF STRING
`s 127 -- what can the Tribunal order` `it is a representative complaint` `the Tribunal found it substantiated` MEANS
    IF (NOT `the Tribunal found it substantiated`)
    THEN LIST "dismiss the complaint (s 127(a))"
    ELSE IF `it is a representative complaint`
         THEN LIST "an order enjoining the respondent from continuing or repeating the unlawful conduct (s 127(b)(ii))", "an order declaring void a contract or agreement made in contravention of the Act (s 127(b)(iv))", "decline to take any further action (s 127(b)(v))"
         ELSE LIST "damages of up to $40,000 for loss or damage suffered by reason of the respondent's conduct (s 127(b)(i))", "an order enjoining the respondent from continuing or repeating the unlawful conduct (s 127(b)(ii))", "an order that the respondent perform a reasonable act or course of conduct to redress your loss or damage (s 127(b)(iii))", "an order declaring void a contract or agreement made in contravention of the Act (s 127(b)(iv))", "decline to take any further action (s 127(b)(v))"

@export What is the most the State Administrative Tribunal can order the respondent to pay me in damages on my substantiated complaint (Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) s 127(b)(i))? Damages compensate for the loss or damage you suffered because of the respondent's conduct, up to $40,000; none on a representative complaint.
GIVEN `my loss or damage` IS A NUMBER @desc The amount, in Australian dollars, of the loss or damage you suffered by reason of the respondent's conduct, as the Tribunal assesses it
      `it is a representative complaint` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Is your complaint a representative complaint?
GIVETH A NUMBER
`s 127(b)(i) -- the most damages I can be awarded` `my loss or damage` `it is a representative complaint` MEANS
    IF `it is a representative complaint`
    THEN 0
    ELSE IF `my loss or damage` GREATER THAN `the damages cap`
         THEN `the damages cap`
         ELSE IF `my loss or damage` LESS THAN 0 THEN 0 ELSE `my loss or damage`

-- s 134(1): "A party aggrieved by a decision or order made under section 126 or 127 of the Tribunal, or by an interim
-- order made under section 126, may appeal under section 105 of the State Administrative Tribunal Act 2004." Section 105
-- of that Act (leave, time) is borrowed and not deposited here.
DECLARE `May I appeal` IS ONE OF
    `yes: under s 105 of the State Administrative Tribunal Act 2004 (s 134(1)); that Act sets the leave and the time`
    `NOTHING-kind: s 134(1) speaks only of a party aggrieved by a decision or order under s 126 or s 127`

@export May I appeal the State Administrative Tribunal's decision on my complaint (Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) s 134(1))? A party aggrieved by its decision or order under s 126 or s 127 may appeal under s 105 of the State Administrative Tribunal Act 2004.
GIVEN `I am aggrieved by a decision or order under s 126 or s 127` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Are you a party aggrieved by the Tribunal's decision or order on your complaint (s 127), or by an interim order (s 126)?
GIVETH A `May I appeal`
`s 134 -- may I appeal` `I am aggrieved by a decision or order under s 126 or s 127` MEANS
    IF `I am aggrieved by a decision or order under s 126 or s 127`
    THEN `yes: under s 105 of the State Administrative Tribunal Act 2004 (s 134(1)); that Act sets the leave and the time`
    ELSE `NOTHING-kind: s 134(1) speaks only of a party aggrieved by a decision or order under s 126 or s 127`

§§ `s 165 -- Whom may I tell about my sexual harassment complaint?`

-- s 165(1): "where a complaint alleging that a person has done an act that is unlawful under Division 4 of Part II
-- [sexual harassment] is lodged with the Commissioner under section 83, a person shall not make a record of, or divulge
-- or communicate to any other person, any particulars of that complaint until the Commissioner has commenced to
-- investigate the complaint. Penalty: (a) in the case of a natural person -- $1 000". (2) "Nothing in subsection (1)
-- prohibits -- ... (b) a person from divulging or communicating any particulars of a complaint to -- (i) the complainant
-- or a person on whose behalf the complaint was made; or (ii) the person alleged to have done the act ...; or (iii) the
-- legal representative of a person referred to in subparagraph (i) or (ii); or (iv) the employer or principal of [such]
-- a person ...; or (c) a person who has made a complaint ... from divulging or communicating any particulars of the
-- complaint to -- (i) a person to whom, or an officer of a body to which, the complainant is making a complaint or
-- application in respect of the same matter; or (ii) an officer of a tribunal in which the complainant is instituting
-- proceedings, or to which the complainant is making an application, in respect of the same matter; or (iii) a near
-- relative of the complainant; or (iv) a person from whom the complainant is seeking or receiving professional
-- treatment, counselling or advice". "near relative" (s 4(1)): a parent, child, grandparent, grandchild, brother or
-- sister, or the spouse or de facto partner of the person or of one of those.
DECLARE `Whom I would tell` IS ONE OF
    `the person I complained about`
    `my lawyer, or the lawyer of the person I complained about`
    `my employer, or the employer of the person I complained about`
    `a body or tribunal to which I am taking the same matter`
    `a near relative`
    `a person giving me professional treatment, counselling or advice`
    `anyone else (for example a friend, a colleague, the media or social media)`

DECLARE `May I tell them about my complaint` IS ONE OF
    `yes` HAS `under` IS A STRING
    `no: until the Commissioner has begun to investigate, you must not record or pass on any particulars of the complaint to this person; penalty up to $1,000 (s 165(1): the text's no)`
    `s 165 does not restrict this` HAS `why` IS A STRING

@export Whom may I tell about my sexual harassment complaint to the Commissioner for Equal Opportunity before the Commissioner starts investigating it (Equal Opportunity Act 1984 (WA) s 165)? Until the investigation begins, no one may record or pass on particulars of a sexual harassment complaint (penalty $1,000), except to the person complained about, the lawyers and employers of either side, a body or tribunal dealing with the same matter, a near relative, or a person giving you professional treatment, counselling or advice.
GIVEN `my complaint alleges sexual harassment` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Does your complaint allege sexual harassment (an act unlawful under Division 4 of Part II: in employment, education or accommodation)?
      `the Commissioner has begun to investigate` IS A BOOLEAN @desc Has the Commissioner commenced investigating your complaint?
      `whom I would tell` IS A `Whom I would tell` @desc Whom you want to tell. One of: the person I complained about; my lawyer, or the lawyer of the person I complained about; my employer, or the employer of the person I complained about; a body or tribunal to which I am taking the same matter; a near relative (parent, child, grandparent, grandchild, brother, sister, or a spouse or de facto partner of you or of one of them); a person giving me professional treatment, counselling or advice; anyone else
GIVETH A `May I tell them about my complaint`
`s 165 -- may I tell them about my complaint` `my complaint alleges sexual harassment` `the Commissioner has begun to investigate` `whom I would tell` MEANS
    BRANCH
        IF (NOT `my complaint alleges sexual harassment`)
        THEN `s 165 does not restrict this` "s 165 applies only to a complaint of sexual harassment under Division 4 of Part II"
        IF `the Commissioner has begun to investigate`
        THEN `s 165 does not restrict this` "the bar in s 165(1) lasts only until the Commissioner has commenced to investigate"
        OTHERWISE
            CONSIDER `whom I would tell`
            WHEN `the person I complained about` THEN `yes` "s 165(2)(b)(ii)"
            WHEN `my lawyer, or the lawyer of the person I complained about` THEN `yes` "s 165(2)(b)(iii)"
            WHEN `my employer, or the employer of the person I complained about` THEN `yes` "s 165(2)(b)(iv)"
            WHEN `a body or tribunal to which I am taking the same matter` THEN `yes` "s 165(2)(c)(i), (ii)"
            WHEN `a near relative` THEN `yes` "s 165(2)(c)(iii)"
            WHEN `a person giving me professional treatment, counselling or advice` THEN `yes` "s 165(2)(c)(iv)"
            WHEN `anyone else (for example a friend, a colleague, the media or social media)` THEN `no: until the Commissioner has begun to investigate, you must not record or pass on any particulars of the complaint to this person; penalty up to $1,000 (s 165(1): the text's no)`
